WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.220 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  I have a child  [Music]  this is Justin hey whould filling in for  our reviewer brett todd little things  make all the difference in divinity  original sin - while this role-playing  masterpiece from Larian studios hits the  high notes as well as the low in its  epic saga about divine ascension quieter  moments resonate just as much as epic  God killing scenes but pulling at your  heartstrings is not all that the game  does well Larian has crafted one of the  finest role-playing epics of all time  packed with meaningful choices evocative  writing and superb acting all of which  combine to form a wholly believable  world the detailed and free-flowing  combat engine provides a challenging and  rewarding turn-based tactical battles  and add tension to every action and  every member of your party feels more  like a real individual than the usual  collection of buffs and numbers found in  most RPGs as with its predecessor from  2014 divinity original sin - setting  remains the D&amp;D infused fantasy land of  Rivlin but the clock has been moved  forward centuries so you don't  technically need any familiarity with  the first game you take on the role of a  sorcerer a name referring to those that  draw arcane power from a mystic material  called source but deploy source powers  and bizarre creatures show up to kill  everyone in sight  this because of this you're viewed is a  danger to society by a governing body of  inquisitors who claim to serve the  divine order and protect society by  rounding up and curing sorcerers the  story begins with you and the other  members of your four-person party being  sent off to the island prison of fort  joy with source blocking callers around  your necks you soon realize that you  have a greater destiny to fulfill  however you and the other members of  your party discover that you are God  Wilkin demigods who have a chance to  ascend and basically replace the seven  gods under threat by creatures from the  void that's where I come in  with my help you will be capable of so  much more than strumming a lute for a  few gold coins this epic saga is a big  undertaking expect to use up the better  part of 60 to 70 hours to complete the  main quest line and a good portion of  many of the side quests the story isn't  just extensive though it's detailed and  gripping largely due to how it avoids  good versus evil fantasy archetypes come  in two RPGs while you start off with  persecuted sorcerers on one side and  oppressive Magisters on the other events  soon carry you into a world of  unrelenting grey where most people are  trying to do the right thing yet failing  miserably even the pallid infection that  shows up in the game as heroes turn into  blinkered zealots basically nobody can  be trusted not even your comrades is  only one of you can ascend to godhood  you're left wide open when it comes to  determining a course of action with  nothing in the game forcing you down any  particular road play good play evil play  something in between this approach is  incredibly freeing it lets you guide  your character and party according to  your own moral compass or a lack of one  I'd forgive this soul punishment must  fit the crime this doesn't  [Music]  anyone who marches at the head of an  undead host deserves whatever punishment  they get freedom with character design  and development really boosts this  feeling every hero comes with a wide  range of core attributes plus civil  abilities combat abilities skills  talents source abilities and more five  racial choices blend the expected humans  and dwarves with the offbeat elves who  consume body parts undead who pick locks  with their skeletal fingers and hide  their boney faces to avoid scaring NPCs  you can roll your own protagonist or  choose from one of six provided  characters representing each race each  of which comes with a specialized  storyline that immerses you deeper into  the saga the voice that rings inside me  now is darker than any that came before  almost caused a bunch of my fans to rip  each other to pieces but you can trust  me I've got it under control even then  you're allowed a free hand to customize  everything you're also able to tell  those joining your party what sort of  adventurer you want to employ picking  from a wide range of classes so if you  want to take on say the arrogant lizard  red prince or the sinister elf Sibyl  you're not locked into a set class as  you would be in most RPGs  at a glance combat is not much different  from many other computer RPGs battle is  our turn-based with action points  governing all activities but where  divinity original sin too differs from  its peers is with the importance it  gives to terrain and environmental  elements pools of water can be frozen  into slippery sheets of ice high ground  gives boost to damage and low ground  restricts it enemies turn these  battlefield features into advantages too  as a result battles are damn tough many  play out like puzzles where you almost  have to play through and lose at least  once in order to figure out how the  enemy will hit you and determine a way  to counter their moves for a second or  third or fourth try  but quest design is less forgiving when  it comes to second chances you can and  will screw up but the adventure rolls on  regardless an NPC can be randomly killed  shutting down a quest before it starts  sometimes you simply cannot succeed at a  skill check necessary to move a  particular adventure along failing  persuasion checks is fairly routine  forcing you to figure out another way  forward and damn the consequences where  most RPGs let you push on and experience  almost everything in a single  playthrough it's impossible to  experience all that this one has to  offer in one play or maybe even two or  three quests are not perfect though  the journal system of tracking them  isn't nearly robust enough to keep up  with how many you have going on at any  given time you can't search it and even  worse key elements are frequently not  included in the text descriptions as a  result of this quest confusion you can  spend too much time wandering around  searching for a key location that for  reasons unknown was not noted on the map  some will believe this to be a good  thing that we finally have a serious RPG  that doesn't hold the hands of its  players but this issue seems more like a  disconnect between how quests are  offered up during the game and how  they're tracked in the journal than any  commitment to old-school difficulty in  addition to the expansive single-player  campaign you can also play with friends  cooperatively or dive into an even truer  pen-and-paper role-playing simulation  with game master mode but this is the  kind of game that you're best off  playing online with friends the involved  story and the necessity to use teamwork  in combat make the game too challenging  if your adventuring with uncooperative  strangers  [Music]  from lonely farmhouses through pitched  battles with gods and far-flung  dimensions divinity original sin - is  one of the most captivating role-playing  games ever made its immaculately  conceived an emotion wrought fantasy  world topped by brilliant tactical  combat make it one of the finest games  of the year thus far and it has to be  regarded as an instant classic in the  pantheon of RPG greats  [Music]

